1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:07,177
This is Vinni at about mile race pace, eight times slower than real life, the day after a race.

2
00:00:07,177 --> 00:00:19,251
We judged his stride at the key positions coaches use, and compared him with the 2017 world championship fifteen-hundred-metre final, and, for his kick, with sprinters.

3
00:00:20,150 --> 00:00:23,648
The biggest thing is the leg in the air at touchdown.

4
00:00:23,648 --> 00:00:25,497
Watch his right leg, in red.

5
00:00:25,497 --> 00:00:34,870
When his left foot lands, the right knee is still about forty centimetres behind the other knee, and the foot is half a metre behind his hips.

6
00:00:34,870 --> 00:00:37,972
Most of his leg motion happens behind his body.

7
00:00:37,972 --> 00:00:40,481
Coaches call that back-side mechanics.

8
00:00:41,383 --> 00:00:47,696
Even at full support, with his body right over the foot, the swing knee still hasn't caught up.

9
00:00:47,696 --> 00:00:53,742
And the thigh takes about a sixth of a second after toe-off just to swing back to vertical.

10
00:00:53,742 --> 00:00:55,869
Knee lift is only fifty degrees.

11
00:00:56,750 --> 00:01:03,883
At toe-off he pushes until the leg is completely straight, knee at about a hundred and seventy-two degrees.

12
00:01:03,883 --> 00:01:13,283
Research on running economy links less leg extension at toe-off with using less energy, and a leg pushed far behind has further to come back.

13
00:01:14,183 --> 00:01:15,952
Here's why it matters most.

14
00:01:15,952 --> 00:01:18,507
In his kick, he keeps the same pattern.

15
00:01:18,507 --> 00:01:23,421
The white figure is a world-class sprinter at touchdown, drawn to his size.

16
00:01:23,421 --> 00:01:25,124
His knee is far behind it.

17
00:01:25,124 --> 00:01:33,575
Distance runners don't switch to sprint mechanics on their own, so the last two hundred metres of a race is where this costs him.

18
00:01:34,483 --> 00:01:38,325
He also leans forward about thirteen degrees at mile pace.

19
00:01:38,325 --> 00:01:42,232
Elite milers run taller, and stay upright as they speed up.

20
00:01:42,232 --> 00:01:52,498
What's already elite-level: his knee at landing, his shin close to vertical, and the foot landing a normal distance in front, all matching the world final.

21
00:01:52,498 --> 00:01:56,670
And he speeds up mostly by raising his step rate, like they do.

22
00:01:57,583 --> 00:01:58,948
A bonus: the backflip.

23
00:01:58,948 --> 00:02:03,290
The jump itself is great, straight up, and he lands where he took off.

24
00:02:03,290 --> 00:02:12,841
But his upper body stays leaning back while he rises, and he only tucks at the very top, so he lands about a quarter turn short with almost straight legs.

25
00:02:12,841 --> 00:02:18,796
Fix: knees to your chest right after you leave the ground, then land in a small squat, chest up.

26
00:02:18,796 --> 00:02:21,029
Practise on a mat or with a spotter.

27
00:02:21,917 --> 00:02:22,499
The plan.

28
00:02:22,499 --> 00:02:31,105
One: sprint mechanics for the kick, with A-skips, dribbles, wicket runs and kick-speed strides, plus knee drives for the hip flexors.

29
00:02:31,105 --> 00:02:38,093
Two: at mile pace, pick the foot up under you instead of pushing it back, with a slightly quicker step rate.

30
00:02:38,093 --> 00:02:39,129
Three: run tall.

31
00:02:39,129 --> 00:02:41,458
We'll retest in three to four weeks.
